WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.460
Film Courage: How do you get comfortable telling
people what to do especially if you feel like

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:05.670
maybe they have more experience?

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:11.190
David F. Sandberg (a.k.a. PonySmasher on Youtube):
It can be a bit intimidating.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:17.390
There was some friction actually on the set
of LIGHTS OUT where…with the camera department.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:20.840
There were some arguments about things.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:25.123
Especially like the first day was a bit of
a set back since it was my first movie it

00:00:25.123 --> 00:00:27.960
was like “Oooh, I better not mess this up.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.570
This is my one chance in Hollywood.”

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:30.680
I planned so much.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.189
I made little storyboards.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:35.769
Little diagrams of shot lists and everything.

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:40.960
And then the first day the DP didn’t really
listen to me.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.649
And some people in the camera department had
made like really big movies that they were

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:49.649
talking about like they were working with
Tom Cruise and all of these things and it’s

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:51.559
like “Oh darn…these are…”

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:55.089
They are so much more experienced than me.

00:00:55.089 --> 00:01:03.179
But it took some friction and there was some
friction and it took some time to really be

00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:05.900
like “This is what I want.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:06.900
Put the camera here.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.960
We are going to put the actor here and we
are going to do this.”

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:16.180
And it took me actually getting a little angry
after a few days.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:23.250
But that helped a lot because then it was
more that we were on the same page.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.130
It’s understandable I guess.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:28.570
If you’ve done all of these big movies and
then you are on this movie with this guy who

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:32.869
has never…yeah he’s never even been on
a film set before.

00:01:32.869 --> 00:01:36.070
Well what does he know?

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:42.439
We had an argument about shutter speed where
it turned out that I was actually right which

00:01:42.439 --> 00:01:45.929
felt really good because it’s like “Yeah,
you’ve done this for a really long time

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:50.740
but I know a lot about camera because I’ve
had nothing but time on my hands.”

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:55.340
And Googling and experimenting and things
like that.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.530
And also with actors especially.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:05.499
Like on LIGHTS OUT working with Maria Bello
was kind of intimidating because Holy Shoots.

00:02:05.499 --> 00:02:13.230
And even casting for that role, there were
a lot of women who came in that I’ve seen

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.440
in all of these movies and TV shows and it
was like “Oh?

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.340
They are auditioning for me?”

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:23.540
Which is just surreal.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:33.069
So it was a bit of a challenge I guess upfront
but it quickly got into a good thing because

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:37.810
I mean actors and everyone on the crew they
want to know what we are doing.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:46.120
I mean they want to know exactly what you
want and what the vision is so they just appreciate

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:47.120
that.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:53.349
And even sometimes where there were moments
where I was like I should do another take

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:58.079
but I don’t really want to ask that from
the actors who have done it so many times.

00:02:58.079 --> 00:02:59.150
Or from the crew or something.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:00.739
So I was like “Yes, it was fine.

00:03:00.739 --> 00:03:02.239
We can move on.”

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:06.470
And then you are kicking yourself afterwards
but that’s another thing that I’ve learned

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:10.330
that I’ve heard from crew who was working
on this other movie with this director who

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:16.110
was just doing take after take and they were
working all throughout the night but the crew

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:17.329
didn’t hate him.

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:20.769
Instead it was like “Yeah he really knows
what he is after.”

00:03:20.769 --> 00:03:25.769
So almost like this respect like “Oh, he
has his vision.”

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:26.769
So it’s like Oh?

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:32.469
They don’t hate you if you’re a perfectionist
about something or if you really want to get

00:03:32.469 --> 00:03:33.469
something right.

00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:37.299
It’s almost as if they respect the fact
that you are not going to give something up

00:03:37.299 --> 00:03:40.969
until something is really good.

00:03:40.969 --> 00:03:46.430
Because everyone wants to work on good movies,
they want to be proud of “Yeah, I worked

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:47.430
on that movie.”

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:53.150
No one is going to be like “I worked on
that movie and it was crap but we got home

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:54.360
at 5:00 p.m.”

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.629
You know?

00:03:56.629 --> 00:03:57.629
People want to be proud:

00:03:57.629 --> 00:04:02.251
Film Courage: Do you think it’s about really
owning the fact that you’re the director

00:04:02.251 --> 00:04:05.569
(that’s that hardest part) and then once
that happens and then once that happens the

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:07.540
crew just naturally follows?

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:08.980
David: I think so.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:14.370
As long as you’re not mean about it.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.140
Yeah, that was an interesting experience as
well.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:21.530
Maybe it’s because I am Swedish where we
have this sort of mentality of “Oh, don’t

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:23.410
think that you are more than anyone else.”

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:26.600
We are all sort of here [gestures to on the
same level].

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:32.380
To come here and see how directors are kind
of spoiled or kind of treated.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:39.970
Like we had this meeting with someone and
I walk in (I was sort of first from our side).

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:45.460
I walk in and meet this woman and it’s like
“Oh hey, how are you doing?”

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:46.980
And she asks “So what do you do here?”

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.570
And I’m like “Oh, I’m the director.”

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:54.720
And suddenly she is like standing up and shaking
my hands and like okay.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.620
Realizing like “Oh?

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.430
Being a director is like a big deal here.”

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:04.700
That was strange because I’m not used to
that and I’m still not used to that.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:10.710
I mean it’s so surreal to see things like
just the other day I was talking about things

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:14.190
like signing autographs and I was like why
would anyone want my autograph.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:20.030
But then I was talking to my manager and then
he went to Ebay and see if my autographs were

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:21.610
on there and they were.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:22.660
Film Courage: Wow!

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:24.750
David: That is insane.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:30.250
People are selling my scribbles?

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:35.850
I don’t know if I’ll ever get used to
that thing of like “He’s that guy!”

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:36.850
It’s like “What?

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:41.120
I’m that Youtube guy who made that thing
with the big parts.”

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:42.790
Yeah, it’s weird.

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:47.310
Film Courage: So that being said, what do
you think makes a great director?

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:52.780
Aside from there’s a pecking order on set
(sorry…bad pun there)?

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:58.280
David: I think communication and clarity of
vision.

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:01.970
And at first when I came here it was like
“Everyone needs to have a vision.

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:04.320
And I don’t know if it was like a language
thing or whatever.”

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.750
But I was like “Oh what do you mean vision?

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:09.990
What is that?

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.450
Just how it looks?

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:19.560
But yes, knowing how it looks, how it feels,
and a lot of times if you’re pitching things

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:24.120
you have to have comps or “It’s like this
thing or this thing or whatever.”

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:30.800
So it’s just like having a clarity of what
you what and being able to communicate that

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.680
and putting what the pictures that you have
in your head into someone else’s head.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:43.910
And some times for me words can’t communicate
properly and then you have to draw things

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:50.080
or cut together things from other movies or
create something just to sort of “This is

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.400
what I’m after.”

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:54.590
But I think that’s the most thing.

00:06:54.590 --> 00:07:01.410
Film Courage: At one point for LIGHTS OUT
movie didn’t you take your Black Magic pocket

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:05.700
camera and you kind of used some of your own
shots or did you do a storyboard?

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:09.160
David: Yes, I did all kind of things.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:15.870
I even did some of the visual effects shots
that wound up in the film.

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:20.590
Which I’ve always had a big interest in
visual effects and it was kind of cool to

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:23.410
be able to do that on my own.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:27.900
I wished I known that I could do that when
we were filming because there was a lot of

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.050
“Oohhh, I want to do this thing.”

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:34.210
And then I was told “Well that was a VFX
shot and then that will cost this and this

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:35.210
and this.”

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:40.460
But then once I was in post I found out from
the editor that the VFX guys don’t have

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:46.090
a union so anyone can do VFX in the movie
which was…it’s a bummer for anyone who

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:51.110
does VFX because they really need a union
but it was cool for me because it was like

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:55.110
“Oh wow, I can do all of these things myself
and get it just the way I want it to.”

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:59.540
Because even with the VFX it was like “Oh,
that’s not quite what I want, I’ll just

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:02.650
do it myself because it’s quick and easy.”

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:05.260
But whatever, yes.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:10.280
I did as much as I could myself because part
of it because I just enjoy doing it.

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:16.450
Film Courage: How does this feel sitting in
the editing bay knowing that Yes this is “my

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:22.360
movie” but in some sense maybe you won’t
have the ultimate say and before [with smaller

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:23.510
indie films] you did?

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:32.140
David: Well that’s the thing, it is very
collaborate and it’s dangerous to have an

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:38.870
ego where it’s like “It has to be my thing,”
because sometimes what your thing is might

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:41.789
not be what is actually best.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:47.040
And the good thing about LIGHTS OUT movie
was that when we made the short it was only

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.819
supposed to be a short film.

00:08:48.819 --> 00:08:53.670
I think it would have been much harder to
make a movie where “Oooh, this has been

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:54.910
my baby for so long.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:56.639
It’s been my dream project for so long.

00:08:56.639 --> 00:08:58.320
It has to be this.”

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:04.649
Because not just because of opinions of others
but things never turn out the way you want

00:09:04.649 --> 00:09:08.230
them to for so many reasons.

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:13.670
It was easy if like someone had a better idea
or if the studio had a note where it was actually

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:17.950
like “Oh that’s cool, let’s do that.”

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:21.709
It was easier to do that when it wasn’t
your precious baby.

00:09:21.709 --> 00:09:30.939
That’s another thing I’ve noticed like
reading stuff online is that the studios are

00:09:30.939 --> 00:09:33.110
only out to sabotage movies.

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:39.029
Like the filmmaker he has this idea and he
just needs to get through this gauntlet of

00:09:39.029 --> 00:09:42.110
a studio trying to ruin it in every way.

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:47.389
Then coming here you realize “Oh the studio
isn’t just people in suits trying to ruin

00:09:47.389 --> 00:09:48.389
movies.”

00:09:48.389 --> 00:09:56.060
I mean sometimes it can be that but it’s
people that want to make movies and they can

00:09:56.060 --> 00:10:00.959
have great ideas and the great thing about
being the director is that you get credit

00:10:00.959 --> 00:10:01.959
for it.

00:10:01.959 --> 00:10:08.399
So if someone else comes with a better idea
than you have, take it you know?

00:10:08.399 --> 00:10:14.881
So that was good to know like “Okay, not
everyone in Hollywood is trying to ruin movies

00:10:14.881 --> 00:10:20.199
and just make money,” because yes the ultimate
goal is to make money because it’s a business

00:10:20.199 --> 00:10:23.589
but again people want to be proud of things.

00:10:23.589 --> 00:10:28.269
People want to say “Yes, that movie you
love, we made that.”

00:10:28.269 --> 00:10:36.439
Even though rather times people would rather
take that then “Yes, this movie made a bunch

00:10:36.439 --> 00:10:40.930
of money but it’s terrible and laughing
stock.”

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:44.500
So yes, there is a lot of people who want
to make good stuff.

